 ## Overview
-Base Renderer Geometry renders voxel data by converting occupied voxel indices into explicit geometric primitives (cubes, squares, or lines) and submitting them to the graphics pipeline for rasterization [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial]. It visualizes scalar field data by treating voxels as discrete 3D objects rather than volume textures [G]. It differs from [Voxel Fragment Shader Rendering](5849-voxel-fragment-shader-rendering.md) by generating geometry in CPU-side or vertex-shader-side buffers rather than computing ray-marching in the fragment shader, and from [Splatting and Point-based Rendering](4975-splatting-and-point-based-rendering.md) by using explicit polygonal faces instead of billboards [G].
+Base Renderer Geometry renders voxel data by converting occupied voxel indices into explicit geometric primitives (cubes, squares, or lines) and submitting them to the graphics pipeline for rasterization [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial]. It visualizes scalar field data by treating voxels as discrete 3D objects rather than volume textures [G]. It differs from [Voxel Fragment Shader Rendering](5849-voxel-fragment-shader-rendering.md) by generating geometry in CPU-side or vertex-shader-side buffers rather than computing ray-marching in the fragment shader [G].
 
